Z||000000||William||10-21-2000||11:39 AM||||Help! how does this thing come off? my books are useless here. It appears the slave comes off with the bracket? The top bolt is out but I can't even see the bottom one? Also I can't find the bolts to remove the slave heat sheild. the top heatsheild is still on too. I have the bolts out in the front but can't even see/ fell the back ones. Also, does the line need to come off first? I can find no more than 1/2 an inch to swing the wrench. I can't believe it's this tough to do. Thanks guys, i'm holding tools waiting for a response...||||reg||1
Z||000001||William||10-21-2000||12:06 PM||||Found the other bracket bolt hidden below the bracket but, I have still been fighting the slave heat shield for an hour...whats the trick here?<p>[This message has been edited by William (edited 10-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||William||10-21-2000||12:37 PM||||Found the heat sheild bolt but the whole shield turns when I loosen it. Is the bolt welded to the shield? Come on guy's someone chime in here...||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wade87GT||10-23-2000||02:36 PM||||The heat shield has one bolt hole and the other side is slotted. So you can expect the shield to rotate, depending on which bolt you loosen first. Actually, this design really makes it easier when you're putting it back together.||||reg||1
Z||000004||William||10-23-2000||03:43 PM||||Thanks for the response Wade. What I had to do was wiggle the shield up and down until it broke into.  It was being held by a stud and the slotted shield was captured between two nuts on that stud. What I discoverd was that instead of the first nut breaking loose, the entire stud moved causing the end on the shield to wrap around the stud as it was rotated. This job only took me 7 hours to do..........:-)||||reg||1
